From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: explorer: the taskbar honours Settings > Personalization > Taskbar

Settings writes the taskbar's options where Windows keeps them and sends
WM_SETTINGCHANGE "TraySettings"; the taskbar ignored all of them. Now:

- Position (HKCU\Software\Stained Glass\Taskbar Position, ABE_*): bottom,
  top, left or right; a vertical bar is 62 px wide (48 with small buttons),
  its buttons square, the clock and tray icons at its foot.
- Auto-hide (AutoHide there): the bar tucks into its edge, 2 px left on the
  screen, when the pointer has been away 600 ms (not while Start or a menu is
  open) and comes back when the pointer reaches the edge; it reserves no work
  area and stays above the windows. What it uncovers is repainted (the
  desktop does not repaint by itself, see 0069).
- Explorer\Advanced: TaskbarSmallIcons (a 30 px bar, the time alone),
  TaskbarGlomLevel (0 always combine and hide labels, 1 when the bar is full,
  2 never -- the default here, as before): combined buttons are one icon per
  program (by executable) with a stacked edge when it has several windows,
  and a click offers them in a menu; TaskbarAl 1 centres the buttons with
  Start; ShowTaskViewButton.
- Search (...\Search SearchboxTaskbarMode): 1 a search button, 2 Windows 10's
  "Type here to search" box; either opens Start ready for typing
  (SgStartPanel, WM_USER+10 with wparam 1).
- SHAppBarMessage answers from it: ABM_GETSTATE (ABS_AUTOHIDE when set --
  it was hard-wired on), ABM_GETTASKBARPOS (the real edge and rectangle --
  it said a 1 px bar at the bottom), ABM_GETAUTOHIDEBAR, and ABM_SETSTATE
  turns auto-hide on and off (saved where Settings reads it). ABM_SETSTATE
  is added to shellapi.h.
- The work area is appbar.c's (0182) for both: the screen less the taskbar
  on its edge (nothing while it hides itself) and every appbar's space --
  0182 took the bar to be at the bottom, and the bar set the work area
  without the appbars.
- The buttons follow windows being shown, hidden, renamed and brought forward
  (WinEvents): a window is usually shown after the notification of its
  creation arrives, so it could stay without a button until something else
  changed.
- A button's icon is its program's, from its executable: another process's
  icon handles cannot be drawn here (Wine's icons are per process), so the
  buttons had none.

Gate: test/taskbar-gate.sh (23 checks; stock fails 21).
